        <span>Mind Sports Analysis</span>
      </div>
      <h3 class="text-base sm:text-xl font-bold text-[#171717] dark:text-white">
        Chess as the Ultimate Mind Sport: Perfect vs. Imperfect Information
      </h3>
      <p>
        Across cultures, tabletop board and card games—such as Shogi, Go, Poker, and Mahjong—have captivated strategic minds for centuries. Today, thinkers, gamers, and competitive players increasingly turn to chess to build mental resilience, pattern recognition, and long-range tactical planning.
      </p>

      <div class="space-y-3 pt-1">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-sm text-[#171717] dark:text-white">
          The Fundamental Divide: Perfect vs. Imperfect Information
        </h4>
        <p>
          In game theory, card and tile games (such as Mahjong or Poker) are classified as <strong>imperfect information games</strong>. Opponents' hands and future deck draws remain concealed, requiring players to manage probabilities, hedge risks, and make calculated guesses under uncertainty.
        </p>
        <p>
          In contrast, <strong>Chess</strong> is a game of <strong>complete, perfect information</strong>. All 64 squares and 32 pieces are fully visible on the board at every moment. There are no dice, no random card draws, and no hidden factors. Outcomes depend entirely on logical calculation, strategic foresight, and precise tactical execution.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-3 pt-1">
        <h4 class="font-bold text-sm text-[#171717] dark:text-white">
          Why Strategy Enthusiasts Thrive in Chess
        </h4>
        <p>
          Despite differing elements of chance, the underlying logic of high-level play shares common ground. Much like discarding inefficient tiles or managing hand equity in tabletop games, chess demands meticulous <strong>piece coordination</strong>—repositioning knights and bishops to exert maximum board pressure. Weighing an aggressive gambit against positional safety is the purest exercise in risk versus reward.
        </p>
        <p>
          For players who crave the intellectual thrill of triumphing through sheer strategy, depth, and creative calculation with zero luck involved, <strong>Chess Web</strong> provides the ultimate online arena.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

